## METHODS Dataset

Our cohort consisted of 2234 individuals, subdivided into a group of healthy controls (n = 448 in the training set, n = 112 in the held-out testing set) and a group of 1674 participants clinically diagnosed with psychiatric disorders, including ADHD (n = 650), ASD (n = 576), ANX (n = 216), and LD (n = 232). The data were aggregated from /uniFB01 ve distinct studies: the Healthy Brain Network Dataset ( HBN ) [43, 44] (https://fcon\_1000.projects.nitrc.org/ indi/cmi\_healthy\_brain\_network/MRI\_EEG.html), Multimodal Resource for Studying Information Processing in the Developing Brain ( MIPDB ) (http:// fcon\_1000.projects.nitrc.org/indi/cmi\_eeg/eeg.html) [44], Autism Biomarker Consortium for Clinical Trials Dataset ( ABCCT ) (https://nda.nih.gov/ edit\_collection.html?id = 2288) [45], Multimodal Developmental Neurogenetics of Females with ASD ( femaleASD ) (https://nda.nih.gov/ edit\_collection.html?id = 2021) [46], and LausanneASD . Subjects included in this study were aged between 5 and 18 years old (mean = 9.99 ± 3.06; 45% M, Fig. S1). Resting-state HD-EEG (128-channels) data were recorded while participants had their eyes open.

Ethics approval and consent to participate . Ethical oversight was provided by the Chesapeake Institutional Review Board, Yale Institutional Board (Yale, SCRI) and the Commission Cantonale d ' Ethique de la Recherche sur l ' e ̂ tre humain (CER-VD, Switzerland). All procedures performed were in accordance with the ethical standards of the 1964 Helsinki Declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. All participants (or their legal guardians) provided written informed consent prior to inclusion in the study. For a comprehensive overview of the datasets, please refer to the Supplementary Materials.

